problem link: https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal/description/
Intuition
The preorder traversal follows:

Root → Left → Right

Therefore, the first element of preorder is always the root of the current subtree.

The inorder traversal follows:

Left → Root → Right

The position of the root in inorder tells us how the tree is divided:

Elements before the root belong to the left subtree.
Elements after the root belong to the right subtree.

To avoid repeatedly searching for the root position in inorder, we store the indices of inorder elements in a hashmap.

Approach
Create a hashmap storing the index of each value in the inorder array.
Maintain a pointer idx pointing to the current element in preorder.
Recursively construct the tree:
Take preorder[idx] as the root.
Increment idx.
Find the root's position in inorder using the hashmap.
Build the left subtree using the left part of inorder.
Build the right subtree using the right part of inorder.
Return the root node.

Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n)

Each node is created once.
Hashmap lookup takes O(1).

Space Complexity: O(n)

Hashmap stores n nodes.
Recursive stack takes O(h) space.

Code
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def buildTree(self, preorder: List[int], inorder: List[int]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        inorder_map = {}
        for i in range(len(inorder)):
            inorder_map[inorder[i]] = i
        idx = 0
        def fun(low,high):
            nonlocal idx
            if low > high:
                return None
            root = TreeNode(preorder[idx])
            mid = inorder_map[preorder[idx]]
            idx += 1
            root.left = fun(low , mid - 1)
            root.right = fun(mid+1,high)
            return root
        return fun(0,len(inorder)-1)
